Hosting and analysis are separate. GitHub Pages hosts the application’s static files—the HTML, styles and JavaScript from the dist folder. The browser downloads these files to run the application. Selecting a local delivery folder does not publish that folder or add its contents to GitHub.

In the implementation reviewed, the browser reads the selected files locally. Parsing, customer/payee mapping, duplicate detection and dashboard calculations run in browser memory on the user’s computer. The analysis workflow has no file-upload or remote-processing API calls.

The analysis.worker-….js entry that appears after clicking Perform analysis is the application loading its analysis engine. A Web Worker runs this code in the background on the same computer so the dashboard remains responsive. Loading that script is a download of application code; it is not, by itself, an upload of customer data.

The hosted application does make network requests to retrieve its code and styles, so “no internet communication” would not be an accurate description. The distinction is that the reviewed implementation processes the selected customer files locally rather than transmitting them for analysis.
